WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.360
"The Best Time to Workout
for Weight Loss"

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.059
What is the optimal exercise
timing for weight loss?

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:18.220
Is it better to exercise
in the morning or the evening?

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.309
Before breakfast
or after breakfast?

00:00:20.309 --> 00:00:23.199
There was a Nobel Prize-
winning exercise physiologist

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:27.400
who said he always ran a mile
every morning before breakfast.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.805
Was he right? Let’s find out.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.040
More than a dozen experiments
have been published

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.360
comparing the amount
of fat burned

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.559
in a fasted versus fed state,
and every single one

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:41.160
found more fat was burned
on an empty stomach.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.840
On average, a single bout of low-
to-moderate intensity activity

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.539
before a meal burned off
three grams more fat

00:00:47.539 --> 00:00:49.710
than the same amount
of exercise after a meal.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:53.260
That’s about three-quarters
of a pat of butter’s worth of fat,

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.719
enough to improve
insulin sensitivity.

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:58.539
The same amount of exercise,
but more fat loss,

00:00:58.539 --> 00:01:00.636
all because of timing.

00:01:00.636 --> 00:01:03.519
Now, just because you burn
more fat while you’re exercising

00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:05.413
doesn’t necessarily
mean you end up

00:01:05.413 --> 00:01:07.580
with less fat
at the end of the day.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.610
Maybe your body offsets
the extra fat loss

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:13.040
that occurs during exercise
with a little extra fat storage

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.080
when you finally do eat,
balancing it out.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.200
Researchers in Japan set out
to investigate the possibility

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.939
that your body makes
up for it later

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:24.610
by measuring 24-hour fat
balance after 100 minutes

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:28.680
of running either before
breakfast or after lunch.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.380
On the exercise-after-lunch day,

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.110
they burned a total
of 608 calories of fat

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:34.940
over the course of that day.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.970
In contrast, on the exercise-
before-breakfast days,

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:40.330
in the same 24-hour period
they burned through

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:45.360
nearly 90 percent more,
1,142 calories of straight fat.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.060
So, the next day, they woke up

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.700
with about a quarter
cup of fat less

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.939
after the same amount
of exercise.

00:01:51.939 --> 00:01:54.040
That’s remarkable!

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.229
What about just
something like walking?

00:01:56.229 --> 00:01:59.759
Sixty minutes before breakfast,
after lunch, or after dinner.

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:02.442
Over the 24 hours
they exercised in the evening,

00:02:02.442 --> 00:02:04.820
432 calories were burned off.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.400
On the afternoon exercise day,

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.700
they burned off
446 calories of fat.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.860
They also had a control day
with no exercise at all,

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.150
and on that day, they burned
through 456 fat calories.

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:20.010
That’s disappointing—it’s like
they never walked at all.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.540
But the same amount
of exercise before breakfast

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.760
resulted in 717 calories
of fat loss.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.400
Over the course of a day
timing matters,

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:31.261
so much so that when it
comes to an hour of walking,

00:02:31.261 --> 00:02:34.680
exercise increases 24-hour
fat burning only

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.534
when it is performed
before breakfast.

00:02:37.707 --> 00:02:39.989
Here’s the fat balance
graph with no exercise.

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:42.239
You can see the fat storage bump up

00:02:42.239 --> 00:02:44.140
at breakfast, lunch, and supper,

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:47.390
before slowly coming
back down overnight.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.090
Exercise in the afternoon
or evening

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:53.090
and end up with more fat
than not exercising at all,

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:54.590
but exercise in the morning
and at least

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:59.230
your fat balance comes
back to baseline.

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:01.930
All such similar studies
on both men and women

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.190
show we burn through more fat
on the days we exercise before,

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.360
rather than after eating.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.230
After reading the
chronobiology chapter though

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.720
or watching my
chronobiology videos,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.433
an alternative explanation
may spring to mind.

00:03:15.433 --> 00:03:17.540
Maybe it’s just a morning thing.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:19.220
Maybe it has nothing
to do with meals,

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:22.534
and your circadian rhythm
is just dictating the difference.

00:03:22.534 --> 00:03:25.285
No. Exercising in the morning
after breakfast

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:28.920
appears no better than exercising
in the evening after dinner,

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.819
and exercising before
breakfast works better

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:34.180
than immediately after breakfast,
both still in the morning.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.010
It really does seem to be
a pre- versus post-meal effect.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.110
But why?

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:43.230
Carbohydrate is the preferred
fuel of the body.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:44.390
When you eat sugars or starches,

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.510
they get broken down
and converted into blood sugar.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:49.780
After a meal, blood sugars rise
and your muscles are quick

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.189
to snatch it up for fuel
without having to rely

00:03:52.189 --> 00:03:53.850
much on your energy stores.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:56.040
If you instead take a siesta,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.879
and your muscles have
no immediate need for energy,

00:03:58.879 --> 00:04:00.349
the excess blood sugar from a meal

00:04:00.349 --> 00:04:03.000
can be stored in our
muscles in the form

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.000
of glycogen for later use.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.319
Glycogen is just a bunch
of blood sugar molecules

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:10.780
strung together into
a mass of branches

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:11.980
that can be broken off and used

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.110
for quick bursts of energy
any time we need them.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:18.660
If you exercise after a meal,
your muscles can siphon off

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:21.450
some of the extra blood sugar
floating around for energy.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:23.780
Before a meal, your
muscles have to instead

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:26.000
resort to dipping
into your energy stores

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.509
and end up burning mostly

00:04:27.509 --> 00:04:30.000
a combination
of glycogen and fat.

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:33.240
That explains why you burn
more fat during fasted exercise,

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.250
but what about all the extra fat

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.380
burned throughout
the rest of the day?

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.420
Glycogen is more than a store.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.760
Glycogen isn’t just
an energy reserve,

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.590
but acts as a sensor capable
of activating metabolic pathways.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:49.699
Exercising before
breakfast can exhaust

00:04:49.699 --> 00:04:52.509
as much as 18 percent
of your glycogen stores,

00:04:52.509 --> 00:04:55.229
and that depletion can
act as a powerful rallying cry

00:04:55.229 --> 00:04:57.229
to your fatty tissues
to start pulling

00:04:57.229 --> 00:05:01.039
more of their own weight
by breaking down more fat.

00:05:01.039 --> 00:05:03.000
The lower glycogen
stores fall,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.930
the greater the sustained
24-hour fat loss.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.289
How long do you have
to go without food

00:05:08.289 --> 00:05:11.509
in order to trigger this effect?

00:05:11.509 --> 00:05:12.820
Six hours may be sufficient;

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:15.330
so, it doesn’t have
to be before breakfast.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:18.310
If you timed it right, you could
exercise before a late lunch,

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:21.280
or if you have an early enough
lunch, maybe before dinner

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.250
when you get home from work.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.250
If exercise in a fasted
state isn’t possible,

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.757
does it matter what you eat?

00:05:28.757 --> 00:05:31.849
Insulin release after a meal
appears to play a critical role

00:05:31.849 --> 00:05:33.378
in suppressing fat breakdown,

00:05:33.378 --> 00:05:35.301
explaining why lower
glycemic foods—

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:37.780
in this case, muesli
versus cornflakes—

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:40.250
can have less of an effect.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:42.120
Lentils were identified
as a promising option

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.220
for maintaining
athletic endurance

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.270
(which can take a hit
on an empty stomach)

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:49.940
while maintaining more
of the fat dissolution.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:52.509
Lentils are said to be
unlikely to be consumed

00:05:52.509 --> 00:05:56.817
by the general population
due to 'low palatability".

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:01.300
They obviously haven’t tried
my mom’s lentil soup.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:02.900
It's worth mentioning
that although

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:05.919
there’s increased 24-hour
fat burning exercising

00:06:05.919 --> 00:06:07.830
in a fasted state,
the only way to see

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:11.340
if this facilitates
weight loss over time

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:13.949
is to put it to the test,

00:06:13.949 --> 00:06:18.231
which we’ll explore next.

